What you can do now
You can now:

structure .qmd into a project — _quarto.yml, _metadata.yml, output-dir;
build a branded website with navigation, and know why cross-page refs differ from a book;
make builds reproducible (freeze + renv.lock) and publish the output folder.


You’ve built the two axes: one document to a branded PDF (Day 1), one folder to a published site (Day 2). Everything your team project needs.

Learning Outcomes
After this session you will be able to:

author a Quarto document natively (.qmd), with figures, tables, cross-references, and math;
lay a document out — page, margin, columns, panels;
make it accessible — alt text, color-blind-safe colors, a built-in contrast check;
turn it into a cited, branded Typst PDF — no LaTeX.

By the end: one penguins document carried all the way to a polished, cite-able PDF.


Frame: you already write R and have met R Markdown or Quarto — today is about what Quarto adds and how far one .qmd can go, not “what is a code chunk”. We build one document and carry it all the way to a cite-able, branded PDF.

How today works
Two rounds, each the same shape (watch → follow along → your turn):

some slides I just talk through; when it’s hands-on you’ll see a Follow along callout;
each round ends with a hands-on Challenge in the lab (solutions are folded in, so you can self-check);
a break sits between the two rounds.



Frame (once, up front): give the room the map — two rounds, same shape; the Follow along / Your turn callouts flag every switch.

What you can now build
You know the literate-programming idea. In 2026, one .qmd is your source for:

a report (HTML, PDF, Word) — figures, tables, cross-refs, citations;
a presentation (these slides are a .qmd);
a website or book (Day 2) — many pages, one config;
a branded PDF via Typst (a modern PDF engine bundled in Quarto, no LaTeX) — a publication-ready article.


One tool, many outputs, multiple languages: all from one plain-text .qmd you write directly.


Frame (not a line): reframe from “Quarto = R Markdown++, nothing new to learn” to “here’s what you can now build” — this audience wants to go further, not be reassured.

Layouts: body, margin, and beyond
Eyes up: the next few slides are concept, no need to type along.
The article-layout model puts content in the body, the margin, or a zone wider than the body (in the paged formats: HTML, LaTeX, Typst):

page-layout: article / full — the overall grid;
margin content — figures, tables, captions, .aside, footnotes;
outset / inset — a figure or table that spills beyond the body (outset) or widens toward the page while keeping a margin from the edge (inset);
multi-column ::: {.columns} and panels — tabsets + layout-ncol.



Do: gloss outset/inset on first use; the rendered visual examples live behind the Learn more links on the next slide, for anyone who wants the full column vocabulary.

What you can do now
You can now:

author a .qmd natively — figures, tables, cross-references, math, callouts;
lay it out — page, margin, columns, panels;
make it accessible — alt text, color-blind-safe colors, a built-in axe check;
cite it with a .bib + CSL, give it a real title block;
ship it as a branded Typst PDF — no LaTeX.


Next (Day 2): grow one document into a whole project, a website your team can publish.
